    "ingredients": "250g beef bones · 120g rice noodles · 60g beef brisket · 60g onion · 50g beef fillet · 40g bean sprouts · 20g ginger · 15ml fish sauce · 15g limes · 15g spring onion · 10g coriander · 10g thai basil · 8g rock sugar · 5g chilli · 2g star anise · 2g cinnamon · 2g coriander seeds · 2g black cardamom · 1g cloves · salt",
    "method": "THE BLACKEST THING IN THIS POT LEAVES NO COLOUR BEHIND, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE TRICK. ⚖️ Everywhere else in cooking, black means you have failed and started again. Here you are going to burn an onion and a piece of ginger until the outside is genuine charcoal, on purpose, and then throw the charcoal away. Understand why before you start, because a timid char is the single most common reason a home phở tastes like beef stock with noodles in it.\n\nCHAR THE ONION AND GINGER FIRST, AND DO NOT BE GENTLE. Halve the onion and leave the ginger whole and unpeeled. Put them cut-side down straight onto a gas flame, or into a dry pan on the highest heat your hob will give, or under a grill on its top setting. Leave them alone until the cut faces are black — not golden, not spotted, black, with the smell of a bonfire coming off them. ✅ Eight to ten minutes for the onion, a little longer for the ginger, turning it so it blisters all over. ⚠️ If your extractor is weak, open a window first; this smokes.\n\nNOW SCRAPE THE LOOSE ASH OFF UNDER A TAP AND CARRY ON. ⚖️ This sounds like it undoes the work and it does not. The pyrolysed layer you want has bonded into the flesh of the onion; the flaking dust on the surface is spent carbon that will only make the broth bitter and cloudy. Rinse the loose stuff away, keep the blackened body.\n\nBLANCH THE BONES, AND TIP THE FIRST WATER OUT. Cover the bones with cold water, bring to a rolling boil for five minutes, then pour the lot into the sink and rinse each bone under the tap until nothing grey clings to it. ⚖️ That first water carries the loose blood and soft protein that turns a broth muddy. It costs you nothing but five minutes and it is the difference between amber and dishwater.\n\nTOAST THE SPICES DRY, THEN BAG THEM. Star anise, cassia bark, coriander seed, black cardamom and cloves go into a dry pan over medium heat for about ninety seconds, until they smell of themselves rather than of dust. Tie them into a scrap of muslin or a tea infuser. ⚠️ Loose spice in the pot means fishing black cardamom pods out of somebody's bowl, and cassia bark will keep giving long after you want it to.\n\nBUILD THE BROTH AND THEN LEAVE IT ALONE. Clean pot, rinsed bones, brisket, the charred onion and ginger, the spice bag, and 1.5 litres of cold water. Bring it up slowly and then hold it at a bare tremble — a bubble breaking every second or two, never a rolling boil. ✅ Three hours. Skim whatever grey rises in the first twenty minutes and then stop fussing. ⚠️ A boiling broth emulsifies its own fat and goes permanently cloudy, and no amount of straining afterwards brings the clarity back.\n\nPULL THE BRISKET AT NINETY MINUTES. It is tender long before the bones have finished giving. Lift it out, let it cool, and slice it across the grain as thinly as you can manage. If you leave it in for the full three hours it will still be edible and it will taste of nothing, because everything it had will be in the broth.\n\nSEASON AT THE END, AND SEASON IT HARDER THAN FEELS RIGHT. Strain the broth, discard the bones, the onion, the ginger and the spice bag. Add the fish sauce, the rock sugar and salt to taste. ✅ Taste it on its own and then remember it is about to meet 120g of unsalted noodles and a pile of raw herbs — it should taste slightly too strong in the spoon. That is correct.\n\nSLICE THE RAW FILLET WHILE THE BROTH COMES BACK TO A HARD BOIL. Cut it across the grain as thin as you can — a spell of twenty minutes in the freezer makes this far easier. ⚠️ The broth must be at a genuine rolling boil at the moment you pour, not merely hot, because that heat is the only thing that will cook the raw beef.\n\nBUILD THE BOWL IN ORDER. Cooked noodles first, then the sliced brisket, then the raw fillet fanned over the top, then the spring onion. Pour the boiling broth straight over the raw beef so it goes from red to grey-pink in front of you. Bean sprouts, coriander, Thai basil, lime and chilli go on the table, not in the pot — every person builds the last third of their own bowl and that is not a garnish convention, it is how the dish is designed to be eaten.\n\nIF YOU CANNOT GET THAI BASIL, HERE IS THE HONEST SUBSTITUTE. ⚖️ Thai basil — húng quế, the purple-stemmed one that tastes of aniseed rather than sweet pepper — is a specialty Asian grocer item in South Africa and this card is not going to pretend otherwise. ✅ Use a mix of ordinary sweet basil and fresh mint, roughly two parts to one, and if you want to chase the last of it, add the smallest possible pinch of ground star anise to the herb plate. ⚠️ Smallest possible. Star anise is already in the broth and a heavy hand here tips the bowl from aromatic to medicinal.",
