    "trivia": "Every other card in this collection treats a blackened ingredient as a mistake. Phở is the one that instructs it, and the reason is chemistry rather than tradition. Burning is not the same reaction as browning. Browning — the Maillard reaction — needs sugars and amino acids at around 140–165°C and it is what gives roast onion its sweetness. Push an allium past roughly 250°C over an open flame and you leave Maillard behind entirely and enter pyrolysis: the sugars break apart rather than rearrange, and what comes out are pyrazines, furans and phenolic compounds you simply cannot reach at roasting temperature, no matter how long you wait. The smoky, faintly bitter, resonant note under a good phở broth is not smoke from the flame; the onion made it internally. The second half is just as deliberate. Almost every other cuisine that chars something keeps the char — you eat the blistered skin of a Spanish pepper, the black edges of grilled meat. Here the charred onion and ginger simmer for three hours and are then lifted out and binned, and a properly made phở broth is clear pale amber with no trace of what went in. It is an extraction, not a seasoning. The blackest thing in the pot leaves the palest broth on the table.",
