    "ingredients": "60g prawns · 50g pork belly · 40g rice vermicelli · 30g lettuce · 25g rice paper · 20g cucumber · 20ml fish sauce · 15g limes · 15g coriander · 10g mint · 10g sugar · 5g garlic · 5g chilli · 5g carrots",
    "method": "TAKE THE RICE PAPER OUT OF THE WATER WHILE IT STILL FEELS TOO STIFF TO ROLL. ⚖️ This is the whole card and it is the opposite of what everyone does first. Bánh tráng does not stop softening when it leaves the water — it carries on, using the moisture already sitting in the sheet, for another twenty or thirty seconds. Soak it until it feels pliable in the bowl and it will be a wet rag by the time you have laid the filling down: it tears, it glues to itself, and it glues to the board. ✅ One second in warm water, both faces wet, out. It should still feel like stiff plastic in your hands. By the time you have filled it, it will be perfect.\n\nCOOK EVERYTHING FIRST, BECAUSE NOTHING GETS COOKED AFTERWARDS. ⚖️ There is no heat anywhere in the assembly of this dish, so every component arrives at the board already finished and already cooled. Simmer the pork belly whole in salted water for about 25 minutes until a skewer meets no resistance, lift it out and let it cool completely before slicing it thinly. Poach the prawns for two minutes in the same water, drain, cool, and split each one down the back into two flat halves. ⚠️ Warm filling steams inside a sealed rice-paper roll and turns the wrapper slimy within minutes.\n\nCOOK THE VERMICELLI AND THEN RINSE IT COLD AND DRAIN IT PROPERLY. Three minutes in boiling water, into a colander, cold tap running until it is genuinely cold, then shake it hard and leave it to drain for five minutes. ⚠️ Wet noodles are the second most common reason a roll fails — the water has nowhere to go and it goes into the wrapper.\n\nMAKE THE NƯỚC CHẤM AND TASTE IT AGAINST THE ROLL, NOT ON ITS OWN. Whisk the fish sauce, the lime juice, the sugar, the crushed garlic and the chopped chilli into about 60ml of warm water until the sugar has dissolved. ⚖️ It should taste slightly weak on a teaspoon. It is a dipping sauce for something almost entirely unseasoned, and a nước chấm that tastes correct on its own will bury the roll. ✅ The balance to chase is sour first, then sweet, then salt, with the garlic and chilli floating rather than sunk.\n\nSET UP BEFORE YOU WET A SINGLE SHEET. A wide shallow bowl of warm water, a damp board or plate to roll on, and every filling laid out in the order you will use it. ⚖️ Once a sheet is wet you have about thirty seconds of usable time and no hands free — this is a dish that punishes hunting for the coriander mid-roll.\n\nBUILD IT IN LAYERS, AND PUT THE PRAWNS DOWN FIRST AND UPSIDE DOWN. Lay the wet sheet on the board. Place the prawn halves cut-side down in a line across the lower third — they are going on the OUTSIDE of the finished roll, so they go down first and face-down, and this is what gives you that pink stripe showing through. Then lettuce, then noodles, then pork, then cucumber, carrot and the whole herbs. ⚠️ Keep the pile low and long rather than tall and short; a fat roll splits at the first bite.\n\nROLL IT TIGHT, FOLD THE SIDES IN HALFWAY, NOT AT THE START. Bring the near edge up and over the filling and pull it back towards you to tighten — that pull is what makes a roll hold together. Fold both sides in once the first turn is made, then keep rolling firmly to the end. ✅ The sheet seals to itself with no water, no egg and no glue; the starch does it.\n\nSERVE THEM WITHIN THE HOUR AND NEVER PUT THEM IN THE FRIDGE. ⚠️ Cold air is dry air, and a rice-paper roll in a fridge goes hard, cloudy and slightly leathery in about forty minutes. Room temperature, under a barely damp cloth, and eat them.",
