    "trivia": "Bánh tráng is one of the few wrappers in world cooking that is cooked before you ever see it and then dried. The batter — rice flour with a good proportion of tapioca starch — is steamed in a thin film over cloth stretched across boiling water, then laid out on woven bamboo racks in the sun, which is where the crosshatch pattern on every sheet comes from. It is an imprint of the drying rack, not a decoration. That manufacturing history is exactly why the one-second rule works. The starch was gelatinised months ago and then had its water taken away; putting it back is rehydration, not cooking, and rehydration does not stop when you stop adding water. The sheet holds enough on its surface to keep softening itself from the outside in, which is why a sheet that feels ready in the bowl is already past ready on the board. The tapioca is doing the other half of the job: rice starch alone dries brittle and cracks when you roll it, while tapioca stays flexible and, critically, stays sticky enough that the sheet seals to itself with nothing added. A roll held shut by starch rather than by heat or egg is the reason this dish can exist with no cooking in it at all.",
