    "ingredients": "100g rice vermicelli · 100g pork mince · 80g pork belly · 60g daikon · 30ml fish sauce · 30g carrots · 25g lettuce · 20g sugar · 20g limes · 20ml rice vinegar · 15g coriander · 10g mint · 10g garlic · 10g spring onion · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g chilli · black pepper",
    "method": "THE SAUCE IN THE BOWL IS NOT A DIP AND YOU SHOULD NOT TASTE IT LIKE ONE. ⚖️ Understand this before you season anything. A dipping sauce is concentrated because it meets food for a second and then leaves. This one arrives warm, filling a bowl, with the grilled pork sitting IN it — and you will spend the whole meal lowering noodles into it and drinking what is left. ✅ It should taste noticeably weaker on a teaspoon than any dipping sauce you have made, roughly at the strength of a light broth. Season it as a soup, not as a condiment.\n\nPICKLE THE VEGETABLES FIRST, BECAUSE THEY NEED THE HOUR. Cut the daikon and carrot into thick coins or short batons — thick, around 3mm, not thin. Toss them with the rice vinegar, half the sugar and a good pinch of salt, and leave them at room temperature for at least an hour. ⚖️ THICKNESS IS THE WHOLE INSTRUCTION. These pickles are going to sit in warm liquid for the length of a meal, and anything sliced thin will be limp within five minutes. Cut thick and they stay audible to the last mouthful. ⚠️ In Hanoi this is su hào — kohlrabi — or green papaya. Neither is reliably on a shelf here, and daikon and carrot are the honest stand-in rather than the authentic article. If you find kohlrabi, use it and cut it the same way.\n\nMIX THE PATTIES AND WORK THE MINCE HARD. Pork mince, the chopped white of the spring onion, half the garlic crushed, a teaspoon of the fish sauce, a teaspoon of sugar and a heavy grind of black pepper. ✅ Work it with your hand for two full minutes until it turns tacky and starts pulling at your palm. That is what gives chả viên its bounce — stop early and you get a crumbly meatball. Shape into flat discs about a centimetre thick and rest them cold for 30 minutes.\n\nMARINATE THE BELLY SEPARATELY AND SLICE IT THIN. Cut the pork belly across into slices about 3mm thick and toss with the remaining garlic, a teaspoon of fish sauce, a teaspoon of sugar, the oil and plenty of pepper. ⚖️ Two cuts, two treatments, one animal: the patty is soft and springy, the belly slice is chewy at the edge and fatty in the middle. Neither alone fills the bowl.\n\nGRILL OVER THE HARDEST HEAT YOU HAVE, AND WANT THE CHAR. Over coals if you have them, or a ridged pan on maximum. The patties take about three minutes a side, the belly slices two. ✅ You are looking for genuine blackened edges, not an even golden. That char is the only assertive flavour in an otherwise gentle bowl, and a pale bún chả tastes like boiled pork in weak soup. ⚠️ The belly renders fat and will flare over coals — keep a cool corner of the grill to move things to.\n\nBUILD THE DIPPING BOWL WARM AND PUT THE PORK STRAIGHT INTO IT. Whisk the fish sauce, the lime juice, the rest of the sugar and about 150ml of warm water until the sugar has gone. Taste — sour first, sweet behind it, salt last. Add the chopped chilli and a spoonful of the pickled vegetables with a little of their vinegar. Then slide the hot grilled patties and belly straight in. ⚖️ They are not a garnish on top; they sit in the liquid and stay there.\n\nSERVE THE NOODLES AND HERBS COLD, ON THE SIDE, AND DIP AS YOU GO. Cooked, rinsed, well-drained vermicelli on one plate, lettuce and whole herbs on another, and the warm bowl of pork and sauce in the middle. Take a small tangle of noodles, drop it into the bowl, lift it out with a bit of pork and a pickle. ⚠️ Do not pour the sauce over the noodles and do not tip the noodles into the bowl — everything goes soft and the dish loses the one contrast it is built on, which is cold and firm meeting warm and soft.\n\nNOTICE THAT THE LAST MOUTHFUL IS NOT THE FIRST ONE. ⚖️ This is the part nobody tells you and it is the reason the dish is built this way. The sauce is a live thing for the length of the meal: rendered pork fat comes off the grilled meat into it, char and pepper wash off, the pickles keep giving up vinegar and taking on salt, and every dipped noodle carries a little starch back in. It starts as a clean sweet-sour broth and finishes cloudy, rounder, fattier and considerably better. ✅ Drink what is left in the bowl. That is not a permission, it is the point.",
