    "trivia": "Almost every sauce in cooking is finished before it reaches the table. A hollandaise is as good as it will ever be the moment it leaves the pan; a gravy is poured and that is that. The bowl in bún chả is the rare one that is deliberately unfinished when it arrives, and the eating is the last stage of making it. Four things happen to it over twenty minutes, all of them intended. Rendered fat leaves the grilled belly and floats, which changes how the liquid coats a noodle — fat carries aromatics onto the tongue and holds them there longer than water can. Char and black pepper wash off the meat and darken it. The pickled daikon keeps trading with its surroundings by simple osmosis, giving vinegar out and taking salt in, so the bowl grows sourer at the edges. And every strand of vermicelli lowered into it leaves a little surface starch behind, which thickens the liquid very slightly and makes it cling. A bowl tasted at the start and a bowl tasted at the end are measurably different things, and the second one is better — which is why the sauce is mixed weak in the first place. Mix it to dipping strength and by the end of the meal it is undrinkable.",
    "howThisFeels": "Smoke off the grill, a plate of cold noodles, and a warm bowl in the middle that quietly gets better every time you go back to it.",
