    "ingredients": "150ml coconut milk · 120g bananas · 25g sugar · 20g tapioca pearls · 15g peanuts · 5g sesame seeds · salt",
    "method": "THE BANANA DECIDES THIS DISH BEFORE YOU TURN THE HOB ON. ⚖️ There is no technique here that rescues an underripe banana and no technique that saves a collapsed one. You want a skin that is deep yellow and heavily freckled with brown, soft to a gentle squeeze, but not yet grey or split. ✅ If yours are still pale-yellow and firm, put them in a paper bag overnight with an apple and they will be ready tomorrow — that is not a folk trick, and the trivia on this card explains exactly why it works.\n\nSOAK THE TAPIOCA PEARLS COLD, THEN COOK THEM SEPARATELY. Cover them in cold water for 20 minutes, then boil them in plenty of plain water for about 10 minutes until nearly clear with a small white dot left in the middle, and drain. ⚠️ DO NOT COOK THE PEARLS IN THE COCONUT MILK. Tapioca sheds an enormous amount of free starch as it cooks, and doing it in the milk turns the whole dessert into glue. Cook them in water you are going to throw away, and rinse them.\n\nWARM THE COCONUT MILK — WARM IT, DO NOT BOIL IT. Coconut milk into a pot with the sugar and a real pinch of salt, over a gentle heat, stirring until the sugar dissolves. ⚖️ Hold it below a simmer. Coconut milk is an emulsion of fat in water held together by its own proteins, and a hard boil breaks it — the fat separates into an oily film on top and the texture goes grainy underneath. ✅ Steam rising with no bubbles breaking is exactly right.\n\nSALT IT PROPERLY, BECAUSE THIS IS THE STEP EVERYONE SKIPS. A pinch that feels slightly too much for a dessert. ⚖️ Coconut and banana are both sweet and both soft, and with no salt and no acid the whole bowl reads as flat and faintly cloying by the fourth spoon. The salt is not there to be tasted; it is there to make the sweetness legible.\n\nCUT THE BANANAS THICK AND ADD THEM LAST. Diagonal slices about 2cm — thick, because they are going to soften considerably and thin coins will simply dissolve. In they go, and then five minutes at that same bare warmth, no stirring beyond a gentle push. ✅ They are ready when the edges have gone translucent and a slice yields to a spoon without falling apart.\n\nTOAST THE PEANUTS AND SESAME DRY, AND DO IT WHILE THE BANANAS SIT. Both in a dry pan over medium heat, moving constantly, until the sesame starts to pop and the peanuts smell roasted. Tip them straight out of the pan onto a cold plate. ⚠️ Both carry on cooking in a hot pan off the heat and both go bitter within about thirty seconds of looking perfect. Chop the peanuts roughly once cool.\n\nSERVE IT WARM, IN A BOWL, WITH THE CRUNCH GOING ON AT THE TABLE. Stir the drained tapioca through, spoon it out, and scatter the peanuts and sesame over each bowl as it goes down. ⚠️ Put the nuts on early and they soften into the milk, and then the only texture contrast in the bowl is gone. ⚖️ This is a warm dessert and it is meant to be — chilled, the coconut fat firms up and coats the roof of your mouth, which is a different and much worse experience.",
