    "trivia": "A banana is one of the few things in a kitchen that keeps making decisions after it has been picked, and it does so by talking to the fruit around it. Bananas are climacteric fruit: they produce a gas called ethylene, and ethylene is a plant hormone that triggers the ripening machinery — enzymes that break long-chain starch down into sugars, and others that dismantle the pectin holding the cell walls rigid, which is why a ripening banana gets sweeter and softer at the same time and by two separate mechanisms. The trick is that ethylene triggers more ethylene production. One banana starting to ripen releases gas that pushes its neighbours to start too, which is the whole reason a bunch goes from green to spotted almost together and why one overripe banana genuinely does drag the rest of the bowl with it. Paper bags work because they trap the gas around the fruit while still letting moisture escape, and adding an apple works because apples are strongly climacteric and generous ethylene producers. Plastic traps the water as well and rots the fruit before it ripens. The commercial trade runs on the same chemistry deliberately: bananas are shipped hard and green at around 13°C, cool enough to suppress ethylene almost entirely, then held in sealed rooms and dosed with ethylene gas on a schedule that suits the shop rather than the plant. The freckled banana this recipe wants is one that has been allowed to finish the job.",
